2026-09-06
        Hardening and clean-up of the Go code base. New greyd.conf options,
        all backwards compatible: config_socket (a unix domain socket for
        greyd-setup, authorised by peer credentials instead of a reserved
        source port), max_config_frame, max_cons_per_source, max_line_length,
        grey.max_domains, setup.max_entries and sync.replay_window (replayed
        sync packets are dropped; a missing sync key is now warned about).
        Each process now confines itself after dropping privileges when the
        new sandbox option is on (the default): Landlock, seccomp and
        no-new-privs on Linux, pledge on OpenBSD (untested by the authors;
        set sandbox = 0 if it misbehaves). Systemd units with hardening
        directives are generated and installed by make install.
        greyd gained -t (test the configuration), --drivers and --version.
        Unknown configuration variables are reported at start up. Internally
        the programs share a typed configuration schema, structured logging,
        transactional database and context-aware firewall interfaces, and a
        hand-written codec for the internal message frames.
        The PROXY protocol handler accepts version 2 (binary) headers as
        well as version 1.

        Observability: greyd answers a statistics request on its
        configuration socket; greyd --stats prints the counters, greydb -s
        summarises the database, and the new greyd-monitor(8) program
        exports everything to Prometheus. SIGHUP reopens the log files.
        greyd adopts sockets passed by systemd (socket activation) and can
        run entirely without root; greyd.socket, greyd-config.socket and
        greyd-unprivileged.service are installed for that. The sandbox
        gained an opt-in seccomp allow list (sandbox_strict) and, on kernels
        with Landlock ABI 4, TCP connect rules that limit the greylister to
        DNS and its database server. Packages (deb, rpm), signed release
        archives with SBOMs, a coverage gate, Dependabot, an architecture
        document and an end to end integration harness (Linux container,
        OpenBSD VM and FreeBSD VM, including a sync protocol compatibility
        test against the spamd of the OpenBSD base system) round out the
        tooling. A native FreeBSD firewall driver, ipfw, joins netfilter
        and pf. The spamd test caught a wire format bug: the port put the
        strings of a grey sync entry two bytes after where spamd (and the
        C greyd) expect them, so grey entries never synchronised with
        other implementations; white and trapped entries were unaffected. The harness found and fixed
        three defects of the port: the netfilter firewall process crashed
        after dropping privileges (keep-caps was set on one thread only,
        and the capability set was narrowed before the drop), multi-line
        blacklist replies written without stuttering used bare LF line
        endings, and the pf driver's pfctl could not reopen /dev/pf once
        unprivileged (it now receives the open descriptor as /dev/fd/3).

2026-09-06
        greyd has been ported from C to Go. The four programs, their command
        line switches, the greyd.conf configuration files, the SMTP dialogue,
        the configuration connection wire protocol and the spamd-compatible
        sync protocol are all unchanged, so the new binaries are a drop-in
        replacement and still sync with spamd and with older greyd hosts.

        Drivers are now compiled in and selected by name rather than loaded
        as shared objects. The Berkeley DB drivers have been replaced by the
        new "bolt" driver, an embedded pure Go key/value store; existing
        Berkeley DB databases are not readable and are simply rebuilt (they
        only hold short-lived greylisting state). SPF support and all of the
        sqlite, mysql, postgresql, bolt & memory database drivers and the
        netfilter, pf & dummy firewall drivers are always available. The
        netfilter driver now uses netlink directly for ipset, conntrack and
        NFLOG instead of the libnetfilter userland libraries.

        A few defects of the C code were fixed along the way: the hostname
        from greyd.conf is used, low_prio_mx is read from the grey section
        (so -M works), proxied connections are matched against the real
        client address, and internal parse errors no longer kill a process.
        The netfilter and pf drivers could not be exercised on real hosts
        during the port and need field verification.

2015-06-08
        greyd now sports sync compatibility with spamd.

2015-04-15
        A host of new database drivers are now available to support different
        use cases & workloads. The blacklist storage is now implemented by
        way of a PATRICIA trie, which makes lookups 1000x faster than previous
        linear search.

2015-02-01
        Added new SPF validation functionality.

2015-01-28
        DragonFly BSD is now supported using the pf firewall driver.

2015-01-24
        NetBSD is now supported using the pf firewall driver. Syncing works between
        any host running greyd.

2015-01-19
        There has been an internal re-shuffle in order to isolate the parts of the
        system that hold a firewall handle to one unprivileged process, with the main
        connection handling process & grey processes communicating with it over a pipe.
        Now the main connection handling process has no privileges, and runs chrooted
        with no direct access to the database (may run as a different user) nor the
        firewall.

2015-01-07
        Greyd now has a new PF firewall driver, and will be included in release 0.2.0.